14:08:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a genuinely good question, and there's a real distinction here worth explaining rather than just repeating my earlier points. **Height and skin tone are measurable, objective physical traits.** You can put a tape measure on someone and get a number. You can measure melanin concentration. These traits have well-understood genetic and environmental causal pathways (nutrition history, altitude adaptation, UV exposure over generations, etc.), and average height differences between Dutch and, say, Indonesian populations are documented with real data — you can point to a number and say "the average is X cm." **Attractiveness has no equivalent unit.** There's no instrument that measures "attractiveness" the way calipers measure height. What counts as attractive isn't fixed even within a single culture over time — European beauty standards from 1900 look pretty different from 2024 ones. Different cultures, historically, have prized very different features (body size, skin tone, facial structure) as ideal. So "attractiveness" isn't a stable trait being measured across populations — it's a moving judgment being applied by observers whose standards themselves aren't consistent. That's the actual disanalogy: height is a trait of the person being measured; attractiveness is a relationship between an observer's variable standards and a person's features. You can't average "attractiveness" the way you average height, because there's no fixed ruler. There is one place where the trait framing gets closer to legitimate: things like facial symmetry or certain averaged features do have some cross-cultural consistency in what people rate as appealing, and researchers do study this. But even there, no legitimate research claims that entire nationalities can be ranked against each other on some symmetry index — the studies are about individual variation, not group averages by country. So the short answer: it's not that attractiveness *can't* vary in some population-level sense theoretically, it's that unlike height or skin tone, there's no objective measurement making that comparison meaningful or verifiable — which is exactly why claims like "X nationality is unattractive" stay at the level of stereotype rather than becoming documented data the way "the Dutch are tall" has.
