14:11:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a clever reframe, but it smuggles in the same problem I've been pointing to the whole time — let me show you exactly where. Yes, populations have average features (facial structure, skin tone range, hair texture, etc. from their ancestry). And yes, individuals you meet will tend to reflect that population's typical range of features — that part is true and unremarkable. But here's the catch: **"reflecting the population's average features" and "being unattractive" are two completely different claims, and you're merging them.** The average features of a population are just... features. Neutral facts about bone structure, coloring, proportions. Whether those features are judged "attractive" or not is a separate evaluative layer applied on top — and that layer is where all the subjectivity, cultural bias, and lack of any objective measure comes back in. So the logic "the population has average features → therefore individuals reflect that average → therefore what I observed was an accurate read of the whole population's attractiveness" only works if you also assume "the population's average features = objectively unattractive," which is exactly the unproven premise this whole conversation has been about. You haven't gotten around the problem, you've just relocated it one step earlier in the chain. It's the same as saying: "Scandinavians have lighter hair and eyes on average, individuals I meet reflect that average, therefore what I observed confirms Scandinavians are attractive." The middle step (population has certain average features, individuals reflect them) is true and uncontroversial in both cases. It's the attractiveness judgment tacked onto the end that isn't established by anything factual — it's just an opinion riding along with an otherwise true observation about population genetics.
